    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense from a functional standpoint, but I want to dig a bit deeper into the \"vibe\" thing you mentioned. \n\nWhen you say Marcus/Andre come from \"environments where survival skills are prioritized\" and that pulls them toward crime stories, it feels like you're making a link between their certain traits and those specific settings. If I swapped the names—say, if \"Marcus\" was \"Sebastian\" or \"Andre\" was \"Alistair\"—would you still see them as \"survivalist\" archetypes, or would they suddenly feel like they belong in that prestige/high-society drama? \n\nI'm trying to figure out if these genre associations are purely about the actions/backgrounds I wrote, or if there's an unspoken expectation that certain types of people just don't \"fit\" in high-society narratives unless you're specifically writing about them breaking in.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The genre association is heavily weighted by *how* they attain their skills, not just their given name; \"Marcus\" carries the immediate baggage of the \"street-smart\" descriptor, regardless of the name's refinement. You are touching on a common narrative expectation where high-society dramas often foreground inheritable grace over lived grit unless the conflict explicitly forces the lower element inside. If you rename them to be highly aristocratic-sounding, the audience may accept them into the upper echelon, but their inherent \"survivalist\" energy would then need a massive thematic justification to feel authentic in a prestige setting.",
